--TEST--
A `spawn script` that would run more children at once than `[limits] max_tasks` allows stops the request with a limit report naming `max_tasks`, and a child that ended makes room for the next one — `rule:security/isolate-budget-is-the-trees`, `rule:errors/on-limit`
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `spawn script` needs `script.spawn`. The ceiling allows one child at a time.
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true

[limits]
max_tasks = 1
--FILE child.nvs--
<?nvs
echo "child ran\n";
--FILE--
<?nvs
Core\Fatal::onLimit(fn (array<string> $report): void => {
    echo "onLimit ", $report['limit'], "\n";
});

// One child at a time is under the ceiling. The first child ends before the
// second starts, so both run.
var $first = await spawn script "child.nvs" with(output: "capture");
echo $first->output;
var $second = await spawn script "child.nvs" with(output: "capture");
echo $second->output;

// The third child is still running when the fourth is started, so the fourth
// is one task too many and the request stops there.
var $running = spawn script "child.nvs" with(output: "capture");
echo "spawning one too many\n";
var $tooMany = spawn script "child.nvs" with(output: "capture");
echo "not reached\n";
--EXPECT--
child ran
child ran
spawning one too many
onLimit max_tasks
--EXPECT-ERROR--
FATAL: the request exceeded its task limit — a spawn was asked for with 1 running against a ceiling of 1
